  "strategy": "Start by importing necessary modules. Use np.random for generating initial candidates and a fallback uniform distribution.\n\n    Define the main function propose_candidate with parameters seed and time_limit, set to default values 42 and 1800.\n\n    Step1: Parse time_limit to 1800 seconds, but adjust to fallback for safety.\n\n    Step2: Check if the provided global sequence height_sequence_1 is available and valid. If yes, use it as base candidate. Otherwise, initialize with a uniform sequence.\n\n    Step3: Implement an optimization loop that tries multiple sequence lengths. For each length:\n\n        a) Generate an initial candidate sequence with normalized elements, possibly drawing from height_sequence_1 if available.\n\n        b) Use an iterative optimization method (e.g., coordinate descent or LP-FW) to minimize the objective function defined in evaluate_sequence.\n\n        c) Monitor time usage and terminate early if time runs out.\n\n    Step4: Within the optimization loop, solve small-scale LP problems using scipy.optimize.linprog. The objective is to maximize sum(g) while ensuring (f*g) has bounded auto-convolution.\n\n    Step5: Incorporate momentum in gradient updates to prevent zig-zagging and use adaptive parameter scaling for faster convergence.\n\n    Step6: After each optimization, record the best candidate and object score. Update the main sequence if better results are found.\n\n    Step7: Project all sequences to the simplex set to ensure non-negativity and valid evaluation.\n\n    Step8: Finally, return the best candidate sequence found, regardless of its length.\n\n    Note: The fallback must be responsive and graceful. Always check boundaries, projections, and scale invariance during the search.\n\n    Time allocation: Assign 600 seconds to each initial sequence generation (e.g., length 500) and 300 seconds to others to balance effectiveness with runtime.\n\n    Example: A walking skeleton built on increasing sequence data, with each section projecting correctly to the objective.\n\n    We must be careful with the algorithm's robustness. For each try, we should protect against possible errors.\n\n    The termination condition will be triggered by the greatest elapsed time exceeding the given budget.",
